  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 AM MST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nFor much of the fall, it seems like the weather has been lagging \nabout a month behind what it should be for this time of year. For \nmuch of September, it felt like August. October was very warm as \nwell and felt more like September for most of it. And for most of \nNovember so far, it has felt like October. And the next three days \nare no exception. \n\nWe still have a gusty breeze in portions of the area, but this \nshould decrease by sunrise. The main feature of the next three days \nand even into the first part of next week will be a sharp ridge that \nwill keep the weather more like October than November and keep snow \nlovers, like some in our office, rather disappointed at the lack of \nthe white stuff that falls from the sky.\n\nTemperatures will continue to average 10 to 15 and some cases close \nto 20 degrees above normal through at least Friday. Wind should \nremain light to moderate through Thursday. Areas prone to southwest \nflow, like Casper, will see an increased southwest breeze on Friday \nas the next system approaches the area. High wind is not expected. \nHowever, with humidity falling to around 20 percent in some \nlocations, elevated fire weather will be possible in some locations. \nCritical fire weather looks unlikely though (less than 1 out of 4 \nchance) as humidity may not reach 15 percent and fuels are turning \nnon critical in increasing numbers of locations.\n\nThe aforementioned weather system, a Pacific trough and cold \nfront, will approach the area Saturday. However, with the ridge \nblocking it, the trough should weaken as it approaches Wyoming. \nAs a result, chances of precipitation and especially amounts are\nlower than yesterday, especially East of the Continental \nDivide. Other than the mountains, there is less than 1 in 6 \nchance of showers East of the Divide this weekend with most \nlocations seeing nothing. The only areas with a greater than 1 \nin 2 chance of a tenth of an inch of QPF or more are the western\nmountains, with no location having a greater than 1 in 2 chance\nof a third of an inch of precipitation through the weekend. The\nmain impact will be some breezy conditions. It will also knock \ntemperatures down a few degrees. However, with the Pacific \norigin of the air, highs should remain above seasonal normals.\n\nTransitory ridging should then keep things mainly dry through \nat least Monday night. The next potential weather maker then \nmoves in for the middle of the week, although there are \ndifferences in timing and exact path of the system. The system \nis approaching from the southwest, so it looks warmer so most \nsnow would fall in the mountains. However, a lot can change over\nthe course of a week. So, for the next week or so, we will \ncontinue to have October in November.",
